course: dictionary
lesson: 6317
title: Mostly: most, but not all
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Mostly» means almost all of something, or the biggest part of it. «The Earth's surface is mostly water» tells you that water covers most of it, but not every bit. The clue is that the word is about a whole made of parts, and most of those parts are one thing.
explain: «Mostly» can also mean usually, or most of the time. «Chameleons change colour mostly to show mood and control temperature» means they usually do it for those reasons, not always. Try swapping in «most of the time» or «usually»: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: Mostly is an adverb, so it tells you how much or how often something happens. It often comes before a noun or adjective: mostly sunny, mostly true, made mostly of. In «mostly males have one», it means most of the group, but not all of them.
fact: Mostly means almost all of something, or the biggest part, but not every part.
fact: Mostly also means usually, or most of the time.
fact: Mostly is an adverb, so it tells you how much or how often.
fact: «Runes were mostly writing, not magic spells.» Most runes were for writing, not magic.
exercise: «The Earth's surface is mostly water.» What does mostly mean here? | Almost all of it is water | *Almost all of it is water, though not every bit | It is cold and wet all over | Only a tiny bit is water
exercise: «Curliness comes mostly from your genes.» Which word could replace mostly here? | Never | Quickly | *Mainly | Not at all
exercise: Nadia says, «We are mostly sunny this week.» What might she mean? | It is never sunny this week | It rains every single day | The sun shines only at night | *Most days are sunny, though not every day
test: Ella says, «Dad mostly cooks on Sundays.» What does mostly mean? | *Dad usually cooks on Sundays | Dad never cooks on Sundays | Dad cooks on every Sunday, no exceptions | Dad cooks the food for the whole house
test: Which sentence uses mostly to mean the biggest part? | *Most of the cake was made of apples | She sat very still | The bell rang loudly | Dogs can run fast
exam: «Muscles get stronger mostly as each fibre gets thicker.» What does mostly mean here? | Only, and nothing else | Never at all | Quietly | *Mainly, for the largest part
